<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.5.1: Reviewing Zero Product Property</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02159</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.5.1: Reviewing Zero Product Property</md:title>
    <md:uuid>3a4c76f3-4f43-4951-99dd-4088743e6954</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Warm Up Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Decide whether each statement is true or false.</para>
      <exercise id="_6992756e-5681-457f-b6a7-c2e9550f74e2" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">3 is the only solution to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Correct! False. There are two solutions because there are two numbers that can be squared to get 9. Both 3 and –3 are possible solutions.</para>
          <para id="para-00004">False. There are two solutions because there are two numbers that can be squared to get 9. Both 3 and –3 are possible solutions.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_4f56e224-75d6-447b-8675-412ccf6c4c52" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">A solution to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>25</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> is –5.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Correct! False. There is no solution to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>25</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because no real number can be squared to get –25.</para>
          <para id="para-00007">False. There is no solution to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>25</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because no real number can be squared to get –25.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="a84ff741-2f65-4dcd-a5eb-bc0584d6f53a" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00008"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> has two solutions.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">Correct! True. There are two factors, 0 and 7, that could make the product of the expression 0.</para>
          <para id="para-00010">True. There are two factors, 0 and 7, that could make the product of the expression 0.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="e4012223-6761-4182-9aa8-3f81e734d6b5" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00011">5 and –7 are the solutions to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>12</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00012">Correct! False. The product of the two factors is not 0, so we cannot solve it using the zero product property (which gives 5 and –7 as solutions). If we evaluate the expression <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> at those two values, the result will be 0, not 12.</para>
          <para id="para-00013">False. The product of the two factors is not 0, so we cannot solve it using the zero product property (which gives 5 and –7 as solutions). If we evaluate the expression <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> at those two values, the result will be 0, not 12.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
